-- title:
--   Law $\mathbb P_q$ of an audit and the expectations $\mathbb E_qC$, $\mathbb E_qN$
-- statement:
--   Fix survival probability $a\in(0,1]$, a side law $\nu$ on $W$ (the law of the old transcript and private randomness) and a target parameter $q\in[0,1]$. Under $\mathbb P_q$ the side variable $w\sim\nu$ is independent of an i.i.d. Bernoulli$(a)$ navigation tape and an i.i.d. Bernoulli$(q)$ target tape:
--
--   $$
--   \mathbb P_q=\nu\otimes\mathrm{Ber}(a)^{\otimes\mathbb N}\otimes\mathrm{Ber}(q)^{\otimes\mathbb N}.
--   $$
--
--   The $k$-th navigation attempt of a run reads navigation entry $k$ and the $j$-th target attempt reads target entry $j$. Because attempts are predictable, each navigation and target transition then has its stated conditional law given the old transcript and the fresh history, and the law $\nu$ of the old data is the same for every target parameter, as the paper requires. The module also defines $\mathbb E_qC$ and $\mathbb E_qN$ as expectations in $[0,\infty]$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathrm{Ber}(r)=r\,\delta_1+(1-r)\,\delta_0$ on $\{0,1\}$ (Lean `Bool`, `true` $=1$); it is a probability measure for $r\in[0,1]$, the only case used.
-- source:
--   Q. Li, When Can Physical Evidence Survive a World-Model Update? Finite-cost re-auditing and reusable policy certificates, review revision of 3 November 2026 (STOC 2027 submission, sha256 a20c437b34115b84982339e9a6fa319cb3a66f66d9d4c474465cbb954c79093d), §4, pp.2–3 (PDF pp.3–4): "Navigation outcomes and target outcomes have their stated conditional laws given the history"; "its entire distribution is generated by a common probability kernel under all compared post-change parameters"; "Fresh navigation and target transitions retain their stated conditional laws given the old transcript and fresh history"

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_Rule
import Definitions.Def_PhysReaudit_run

open MeasureTheory

namespace PhysReaudit

/-- The Bernoulli(r) law on `Bool`: mass `r` on `true` and `1 − r` on `false`.

**Formalization Note** For `r ∈ [0, 1]` this is the Bernoulli distribution; it is only used there. -/
noncomputable def bern (r : ℝ) : Measure Bool :=
  ENNReal.ofReal r • Measure.dirac true + ENNReal.ofReal (1 - r) • Measure.dirac false

/-- The law of an audit under target parameter `q` (Q. Li, *When Can Physical Evidence Survive a World-Model Update?*, review revision of 3 November 2026 (sha256 a20c437b…), §4, pp.2–3 (PDF pp.3–4)): the side variable
`w ∼ ν` (old transcript and private randomness), independent of an i.i.d. Bernoulli(a) navigation tape and
an i.i.d. Bernoulli(q) target tape.

This encodes the paper's model exactly: "Navigation outcomes and target outcomes have their stated
conditional laws given the history"; "[the old transcript's] entire distribution is generated by a common
probability kernel under all compared post-change parameters" (`ν` does not depend on `q`); "Fresh
navigation and target transitions retain their stated conditional laws given the old transcript and fresh
history". Since attempts are predictable, each attempt reads a fresh tape entry independent of everything
observed before.

**Formalization Note** The tapes are `Measure.infinitePi` products, which are probability measures when
`a, q ∈ [0, 1]` (Mathlib returns `0` otherwise, a case never used). -/
noncomputable def lawQ {W : Type} [MeasurableSpace W] (ν : Measure W) (a q : ℝ) : Measure (Omega W) :=
  ν.prod ((Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℕ => bern a).prod (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℕ => bern q))

/-- The expected physical cost `E_q C` of rule `R` with side law `ν`, in `ℝ≥0∞`. -/
noncomputable def expCost {W : Type} [MeasurableSpace W] (R : Rule W) (ν : Measure W) (a : ℝ) (d : ℕ)
    (q : ℝ) : ENNReal :=
  ∫⁻ ω, (cost R d ω : ENNReal) ∂(lawQ ν a q)

/-- The expected number of target observations `E_q N` of rule `R` with side law `ν`, in `ℝ≥0∞`. -/
noncomputable def expTargets {W : Type} [MeasurableSpace W] (R : Rule W) (ν : Measure W) (a : ℝ) (d : ℕ)
    (q : ℝ) : ENNReal :=
  ∫⁻ ω, (targetCount R d ω : ENNReal) ∂(lawQ ν a q)

end PhysReaudit


